BEGIN_PGML Next, add a column to your spreadsheet that computes [`\lvert f(x)-L(x)\rvert\text{.}`] What is the new entry in Row 14?
[_]{abs(exp(0.4)-1.4)}{10}
How do the differences between [`f(x)`] and [`L(x)`] change as you move across the interval from [`-1`] to [`1\text{?}`]
[_]{$radio}{5}
END_PGML
Section::End();
############################################################ # Section ############################################################ Section::Begin("");
BEGIN_PGML For about what values of [`x`] is it true that [`\DeclareMathOperator{\erf}{erf}\DeclareMathOperator{\arctanh}{arctanh}\DeclareMathOperator{\arcsec}{arcsec}\newcommand{\lt}{<}\lvert f(x) - L(x)\rvert \lt 0.1\text{?}`]
[@KeyboardInstructions('For example, if your spreadsheet shows that first times the error is greater than or equal to [`0.1`] are [`-0.7`] and [`0.7\text{,}`] then enter the interval [`(-0.7, 0.7)\text{.}`]')@]**
[_]{$interval}{10}
END_PGML
Section::End();
############################################################ # Section ############################################################ Section::Begin("");
BEGIN_PGML Notice that the curvature in [`f(x) = e^x`] is what makes the linear approximation [`L(x)`] lose accuracy. What kind of simple function might do a better job approximating [`e^x`] than a linear one?
[_]{$popup}{5}
END_PGML
